From: spyworks73 <spyworks73@github.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Jun 2026
Subject: [PATCH] xhci-pci: Restore XHCLKGTEN register on Surface Pro 6 (Intel 8086:9d2f)

The UEFI firmware on Surface Pro 6 (model 1796, firmware 239.779.768)
corrupts PCI configuration register 0x50 (XHCLKGTEN, xHCI Clock Gating
Enable) for the Sunrise Point-LP PCH xHCI controller at ExitBootServices().

The register changes from 0x0FDF6D3F (working) to 0x0FCE6E5B (broken),
causing xhci_hcd: can't setup: -110 (-ETIMEDOUT) on every OS boot without
exception (Windows, Linux kernels 6.6 through 6.19, all live distributions).

XOR of the two values: 0x0FDF6D3F XOR 0x0FCE6E5B = 0x00110164
Specific clock gating bits required by the hardware are disabled by the
firmware at the OS handoff point.

The fix writes back the correct XHCLKGTEN value directly in the driver probe
function, right before usb_add_hcd() is called, with ACPI fully active. This
eliminates the need for acpi=off and allows the Type Cover keyboard/trackpad
and IPTS touchscreen to function normally.

IMPORTANT: The function parameter in xhci_pci_common_probe() is named 'dev'
(not 'pdev'). Using 'pdev' causes a compiler error: 'pdev' undeclared.

This fix requires CONFIG_USB_XHCI_HCD=m (module, not built-in =y). With =y,
the driver initializes before any register correction is possible.

Companion fix: A HCRST skip patch in xhci.c is also required because the
HCRST (Host Controller Reset) bit is permanently stuck at 1 in this device's
silicon (observed: USBCMD = 0x00000003 = Run=1 AND HCRST=1 simultaneously,
which is an illegal xHCI state per specification). See xhci-hcrst-skip.patch.

Tested on: Surface Pro 6 (model 1796), Intel Core i5-8250U, kernel 6.19.8
with linux-surface patches, Ubuntu 24.04 Noble.

Boot log confirmation after patch:
  [    2.891] xhci-pci 0000:00:14.0: SP6 FIX: XHCLKGTEN hardware register corrected in probe
  [    2.934] xhci-pci 0000:00:14.0: xHCI Host Controller
  [    2.941] xhci-pci 0000:00:14.0: new USB bus registered, assigned bus number 1
  [    3.012] usb 1-1: new high-speed USB device number 2 using xhci_hcd

================================================================================
IMPORTANT WARNINGS — READ BEFORE APPLYING
================================================================================

WARNING 1 — LINE NUMBERS ARE KERNEL-VERSION-SPECIFIC:
  The diff hunk below references @@ -650,6 +650,19 @@, which corresponds to
  kernel 6.19.8-surface (linux-surface project). Line numbers WILL differ in
  other kernel versions. If "git apply" fails with "hunk does not apply",
  locate the target manually:

    grep -n "usb_add_hcd" drivers/usb/host/xhci-pci.c | grep "IRQF_SHARED"

  Insert the fix block immediately before that line, inside the function
  xhci_pci_common_probe(). The surrounding context (the goto put_hcd block)
  can be used to verify placement.

WARNING 2 — VARIABLE NAME IS 'dev', NOT 'pdev':
  The function signature is:
    static int xhci_pci_common_probe(struct pci_dev *dev, ...)
  The PCI device pointer is named 'dev'. Using 'pdev' causes a compiler
  error: error: 'pdev' undeclared (first use in this function).
  This is a known pitfall — many PCI drivers use 'pdev' but this one
  does not. Always verify with: grep "xhci_pci_common_probe" drivers/usb/host/xhci-pci.c

WARNING 3 — REQUIRES CONFIG_USB_XHCI_HCD=m (MODULE, NOT BUILT-IN):
  With CONFIG_USB_XHCI_HCD=y, the driver is compiled into the kernel image
  and initializes before any module-load-time intervention is possible.
  The probe function still runs, so this patch IS compatible with =y in
  principle — but =m is strongly recommended for the SP6 use case to allow
  initramfs-level sequencing if needed in the future.

WARNING 4 — SAFE FOR NON-SP6 HARDWARE:
  The fix is guarded by an exact PCI vendor+device ID check:
    dev->vendor == PCI_VENDOR_ID_INTEL (0x8086) && dev->device == 0x9d2f
  This combination uniquely identifies the Sunrise Point-LP PCH xHCI
  controller. The code path will not execute on any other hardware.
  PCI_VENDOR_ID_INTEL is defined in <linux/pci_ids.h> and always available.

================================================================================

--- a/drivers/usb/host/xhci-pci.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/host/xhci-pci.c
@@ -650,6 +650,19 @@ static int xhci_pci_common_probe(struct pci_dev *dev, const struct pci_device_i
 	if (ret)
 		goto put_hcd;

+	/*
+	 * Surface Pro 6 (Intel 8086:9d2f / Sunrise Point-LP PCH) workaround:
+	 *
+	 * The UEFI firmware corrupts PCI config register 0x50 (XHCLKGTEN,
+	 * xHCI Clock Gating Enable) at ExitBootServices(), changing it from
+	 * 0x0FDF6D3F (working) to 0x0FCE6E5B (broken). This causes
+	 * xhci_hcd: can't setup: -110 on every OS boot.
+	 *
+	 * Restore the correct value here, right before usb_add_hcd() is called,
+	 * with ACPI fully active. This eliminates the need for acpi=off and
+	 * allows the Type Cover and IPTS touchscreen to work normally.
+	 *
+	 * Note: variable is 'dev' in this function, NOT 'pdev'.
+	 */
+	if (dev->vendor == PCI_VENDOR_ID_INTEL && dev->device == 0x9d2f) {
+		pci_write_config_dword(dev, 0x50, 0x0FDF6D3F);
+		dev_info(&dev->dev, "SP6 FIX: XHCLKGTEN hardware register corrected in probe\n");
+	}
+
 	return usb_add_hcd(hcd, irq, IRQF_SHARED);
